/-!
# The literal binary-three Sylow interval in the original catalogue

The specified FF marking identifies the actual two-core quotient with
SL₃(2). Its two maximal Sylow overgroups lift through that quotient to
subgroups of the original carrier. They intersect in S∩K, generate K,
have index seven, have characteristic two and have S₃ two-core quotients.
Their images under the literal quotient map are maximal linear parabolics.
The full interval consists of the Sylow subgroup, these two parabolics and K.

Each parabolic is generated by S∩K and its embedded O^{3′}, which escapes
the other parabolic. Both original intersections lie in this same interval.
This holds for every eligible K in L(G₁,S), with the original G₁, M_Z and S;
no ambient C-componenthood or replacement selection record is assumed.
The selected-component applications retain both source primes, the original
FF quotient marking, and the later exchanged applications. Their flag images
use that same marking. The actual-marker application retains original 15.1.14
for its existing producer and final assembly owner.

The final conditional application isolates the three remaining position
facts: neither M_L nor M∩L equals S_L, and they are distinct. These imply
both maximal-parabolic clauses, generation, and escape of the literal
O^{3′}(M∩L). They are not assumed to follow merely from properness.
Block reconstruction, the fixed-seed centralizer and final centralizer
identity remain source obligations, as do the A₆ rows and general Borel
placement. No universal C.1.34 classification is used.

Source: Aschbacher--Smith 15.1.20(2b) and 15.1.22(2), printed pp.1094--1100;
the binary parabolic interval is I.5.4, printed p.420. The imported concrete
matrix model retains the ATLAS attribution in LICENSES/ATLAS.txt.
-/

